A view controller must be able to present another.

The whole NSViewController presentation family was missing. Not stubbed, not partial: grepping
NSViewController.m and its header for presentViewControllerAsSheet:, presentViewController:animator:,
dismissViewController: or presentViewControllerAsModalWindow: returned nothing at all.

iA Writer opens its Custom Patterns editor that way. Measured before this change, with the click
aimed from traces rather than by eye:

    cider: UNRECOGNIZED -[IAEditorPreferencesContentViewController presentViewControllerAsSheet:]

The button worked, the action ran, and nothing appeared.

NSApplication already has beginSheet: and endSheet:, so this only has to build the window that
carries the presented controller and remember which window that was, because dismissViewController:
is handed the controller and nothing else. The association lives on the presented controller.

MEASURED AFTER, same drive: UNRECOGNIZED count for present is 0, and window 26 maps at 390x329,
which is the 390x307 view of CustomPatternsPreferences plus a title bar. The capture shows the
sheet over the Editor pane with its introduction label, an empty pattern table, the add and remove
buttons and a blue Done.

presentViewControllerAsModalWindow: is the same machinery with no parent. No roster application
reaches it, and this does not claim otherwise; it is here because an application that reached for
it would die exactly the way iA Writer did.

WHAT IS STILL WRONG, and it is not this: the add button on that sheet receives its click, at the
right point in the right surface, and sends no action. Measured, in this order: the target binding
IS established with its NSSelectorName option, so cocotron 0138 gave it an action; the click
arrives at the sheet surface at content y 31 against a button spanning 20 to 41, so it lands; and
CIDER_TRACE_MSGSEND on the target class shows 207 messages and not one addPattern:. So the sheet
is presented and the control is wired, and something between the click and the action is not.

--- a/AppKit/NSViewController.m
+++ b/AppKit/NSViewController.m
@@ -1,5 +1,8 @@
 #include <string.h>
+#import <AppKit/NSApplication.h>
 #import <AppKit/NSNib.h>
+#import <AppKit/NSWindow.h>
+#import <AppKit/NSView.h>
 #import <AppKit/NSNibLoading.h>
 #import <AppKit/NSRaise.h>
 #import <AppKit/NSViewController.h>
@@ -442,6 +445,79 @@
  * view ends up in the old one's place and the handler runs, and doing it immediately keeps that
  * contract. Animating it is a separate question from being correct.
  */
+/* THE PRESENTATION FAMILY, none of which existed. iA Writer opens Custom Patterns with
+ * presentViewControllerAsSheet: and the selector was UNRECOGNIZED, so the button worked, the
+ * action ran, and nothing appeared.
+ *
+ * The presented controller owns nothing here, so the window that carries it is remembered on the
+ * controller itself; dismissViewController: has only the controller to go on. */
+static const char kCiderPresentationWindow;
+
+- (NSWindow *) _ciderWindowCarrying: (NSViewController *) viewController {
+    return objc_getAssociatedObject(viewController, &kCiderPresentationWindow);
+}
+
+- (NSWindow *) _ciderMakeWindowFor: (NSViewController *) viewController {
+    NSView *content = [viewController view];
+    NSRect frame = (content != nil) ? [content frame] : NSMakeRect(0, 0, 320, 200);
+    NSWindow *window = [[NSWindow alloc] initWithContentRect: frame
+                                                  styleMask: NSTitledWindowMask
+                                                    backing: NSBackingStoreBuffered
+                                                      defer: NO];
+
+    if (content != nil)
+        [window setContentView: content];
+    if ([viewController title] != nil)
+        [window setTitle: [viewController title]];
+
+    objc_setAssociatedObject(viewController, &kCiderPresentationWindow, window,
+                             OBJC_ASSOCIATION_RETAIN);
+    [window release];
+
+    return window;
+}
+
+- (void) presentViewControllerAsSheet: (NSViewController *) viewController {
+    NSWindow *sheet = [self _ciderMakeWindowFor: viewController];
+
+    [viewController _ciderSendViewDidLoadIfNeeded];
+    [NSApp beginSheet: sheet
+            modalForWindow: [[self view] window]
+             modalDelegate: nil
+            didEndSelector: NULL
+               contextInfo: NULL];
+    [viewController _ciderSendAppearance: YES];
+}
+
+/* The same machinery with no parent, which is what a modal window is. Not exercised by any roster
+ * application, and this does not claim it is; it is here because an application that reaches for it
+ * would otherwise die the same way iA Writer did. */
+- (void) presentViewControllerAsModalWindow: (NSViewController *) viewController {
+    NSWindow *window = [self _ciderMakeWindowFor: viewController];
+
+    [viewController _ciderSendViewDidLoadIfNeeded];
+    [viewController _ciderSendAppearance: YES];
+    [NSApp runModalForWindow: window];
+}
+
+- (void) dismissViewController: (NSViewController *) viewController {
+    NSWindow *window = [self _ciderWindowCarrying: viewController];
+
+    if (window == nil)
+        return;
+
+    [viewController _ciderSendAppearance: NO];
+
+    if ([window isSheet])
+        [NSApp endSheet: window];
+    else
+        [NSApp stopModal];
+
+    [window orderOut: self];
+    objc_setAssociatedObject(viewController, &kCiderPresentationWindow, nil,
+                             OBJC_ASSOCIATION_RETAIN);
+}
+
 - (void) transitionFromViewController: (NSViewController *) fromViewController
                      toViewController: (NSViewController *) toViewController
                               options: (NSViewControllerTransitionOptions) options
